 but it wasn't easy he blames the  government  and people in positions of influence who  get access to critical hospital  admissions  faster than people from his  socioeconomic background or neighborhood  it is very difficult to get an admission  in hospital we couldn't find a free bed  for the last four days we went through  all the hospitals but there was no  admission anywhere  but i managed to get it at this  government hospital here  he isn't the only one but few people we  asked wanted to comment publicly  many are also reluctant to confirm if  they've had to offer bribes for a  hospital admission  bribing or bakshish as it's referred to  locally  is a common administrative practice here  but the reality is the big people here  don't have the hassles we do  they get a good and prompt service  common people like us don't get that  too often the quality of care afforded  the sick and dying comes down to class  she was able to get hospital admission  but died when the oxygen she needed to  live  was turned off since he couldn't visit  he got the help of a hospital assistant  so he could see her  via video call the night before she died  how are you kidding me  i can say this hospital doesn't care  anymore they aren't caring towards covet  patients  patients are all the same hospitals have  been operating beyond capacity  for weeks there are a total of 700 000  beds in public health institutions  across  india estimates suggest another 1.2  million beds are available in private  hospitals  combined that's less than 2 million beds  for a population  of 1.3 billion as the country battles  the world's  largest covert 19 crisis statistics show  there are just  95 000 icu beds and 48 000  ventilators available canada and other  countries  have sent aid money antiviral drugs and  badly needed ventilators  but more help is needed  we went to about four to five hospitals  there they didn't have  any oxygen cylinders there was a lot of  struggle to get one  rural areas are often poorly equipped  but even around the state of delhi  people risk traveling to the capital  city where there are more medical  facilities  to plead for access to a coveted wing  the public health system in india  already has  three tiers which tend to reflect the  rural to urban divide  but even in cities like here in delhi  there are pockets of migrant workers and  daily wages  who would not have the right connections  let alone the facilities to get admitted  to a coveted ward  within a public or private hospital  where this  leaves people like here is a question  that remains to be answered arabella  cibola global news  delhi up next home  on the go what's steering so many people  to live  in their vehicles  queen elizabeth served up some laughs  when she insisted on using a sword  to cut up a cake the dessert was for g7  leaders on the first night of their  three-day summit in cornwall england  the queen was offered a knife but said  the ceremonial sword was  more unusual eventually the duchess of  cambridge stepped in to help  this is the first time britain's royals  are taking part in g7 events  if you search the term van life on  social media you'll find millions of  posts  most photos show a bohemian lifestyle or  sense of adventure  but the nomadic lifestyle of living in a  vehicle isn't always romantic and  carefree  for some it's all they can afford kayla  mclean explains  [Applause]  at this shop vans are converted into  homes  since the pandemic started madison  mcnair and her husband's business  has been nonstop it's been really  incredible  so we're already booking into 2022 and  2023.  van life the lure of freedom and travel  in a self-contained home on wheels  has given rise to a social media  phenomenon which in part  has been fueled by covet 19. for some  it's choice for others it's necessity  rent's expensive jobs are in stable how  do i sign a 12-month lease  if my job won't give me a 12-month  stability these vehicles are  far more about freedom self-control  autonomy this is also another place that  we stay at sometimes  in squamish b.c thomasina pigeon lives  in this van with her 14-year-old  daughter  feels like the way that i'm supposed to  live when i'm in a house i feel really  restricted  while tomasina prefers this lifestyle  she couldn't afford to pay rent  even if she wanted to i don't really  make that much money i own my own  business but  the amount of money that i make is below  poverty level with job losses  skyrocketing rent and the booming  housing market  some people have been forced into  vehicle living and as canada's  affordable housing crisis worsens  tensions between cities and van dwellers  seems to be growing  some municipalities are cracking down on  rvs  trailers and vans illegally parked on  streets squamish is no exception  passing by-law changes that would  prevent people sleeping in their vehicle  overnight  in certain public spaces thomas cena  co-directs an advocacy group  fighting the bylaw i think the bylaws  and the laws in general need to  reflect this growing reality that a lot  of people are choosing vehicles  as homes and it's a trend and it's not  going to go away  karen elliott is the mayor of squamish  she says the amendments are to manage  the tourist population and not aimed at  long-term vehicle residents  it is not targeting a people that call  squamish home  full-time it really is about managing  the influx of thousands  of vehicles each summer and thousands of  people that are looking for a place to  stay  back at the shop there's no slowing down  and the couple knows the van life trend  will continue  out of desire or need ultimately  it's about taking leaps and finding what  works for you and apparently in pandemic  times if you've decided you don't like  your van there is someone that will buy  that van  kayla mclean global news toronto  to learn more about why some  municipalities are now cracking down  on van lifers watch the new reality  tonight